---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Does DKIM encrypt email?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "No. DKIM signs the message so receivers can verify it was not altered. Encryption of the transport is a separate TLS mechanism." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What happens if my DKIM key is revoked?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Mail signed with that selector fails verification. That is why rotation publishes the new selector first and retires the old one only after signing moves." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can I have multiple DKIM records?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Yes, under different selectors. Each selector is its own DNS name, and mail carries the selector it was signed with." } }
  ],
};

import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';

---
<BaseLayout schema={[faqSchema]} title="DKIM Explained: DomainKeys Identified Mail" description="DKIM explained: how cryptographic signatures prove your email wasn't forged, how selectors and keys work, and how to set DKIM up."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"], ["DKIM"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DKIM</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DKIM Explained</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      SPF lists <em>who may send</em>. DKIM proves <em>the mail itself is intact</em>: every message
      is cryptographically signed with a private key, and receivers verify the signature against the
      public key you publish in DNS.
    </p>
    </div>

    

    

    

    
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#how-it-works-end-to-end">How it works, end to end</a></li>
        <li><a href="#selectors-one-domain-many-keys">Selectors: one domain, many keys</a></li>
        <li><a href="#key-strength-matters">Key strength matters</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dkim-the-rest">DKIM + the rest</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">DKIM email header tags vs DNS record tags</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Tag</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Email header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">v=1</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">DKIM signature version</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Email header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">a=rsa-sha256</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Signing algorithm</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Email header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">d=example.com</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Signing domain (the d= tag)</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Email header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">s=selector1</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Selector used for the DNS lookup</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Email header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">bh=</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Body hash</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Email header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">b=</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Signature data</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DNS TXT record</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">v=DKIM1</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Record version</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DNS TXT record</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">k=rsa</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Key type</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DNS TXT record</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">p=</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Public key material (empty p= means revoked)</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DNS TXT record</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">t=y</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Testing mode flag</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">DKIM key strength comparison</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Status</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">512-bit RSA</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Insecure</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Rejected by major receivers</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">1024-bit RSA</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Legacy minimum</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Increasingly weak; migrate</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">2048-bit RSA</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Current standard</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">May need two TXT strings for the key material</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Ed25519 (RFC 8463)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Modern alternative</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Shorter keys, faster signing; receiver support still maturing</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
    <Diagram name="dkim-signing" caption="How DKIM signing and verification work" />

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="how-it-works-end-to-end">How it works, end to end</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your sending server generates a key pair. The <strong>private key</strong> stays on the server; the <strong>public key</strong> goes into DNS at <code>selector._domainkey.yourdomain.com</code>.</li>
        <li>Each outgoing message gets a <code>DKIM-Signature</code> header covering the important headers and body, tagged with the selector used.</li>
        <li>The receiver's mail server fetches that public key from DNS and verifies the signature. Valid = the mail genuinely came from your domain and wasn't modified in transit.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="selectors-one-domain-many-keys">Selectors: one domain, many keys</h2>
      <p>
        The selector is the label in the DNS name (<code>google._domainkey…</code>,
        <code>k1._domainkey…</code>, <code>s1._domainkey…</code>). It lets every service you send
        through publish its own key without collisions, which is also why you must tell any checker
        <em>which</em> selector to look up. Use the <a href="/dkim-record-checker/">DKIM checker</a>
        with the selector your provider documents.
      </p>

      <h2 id="key-strength-matters">Key strength matters</h2>
      <p>
        1024-bit RSA keys are considered weak today; some receivers refuse them outright. 2048-bit is
        the standard recommendation (ed25519 is the modern alternative). Rotating keys is normal,
        publish the new selector before retiring the old one so in-flight mail still verifies.
      </p>

      <h2 id="dkim-the-rest">DKIM + the rest</h2>
      <p>
        DKIM passes are one of the two inputs <a href="/dmarc/">DMARC</a> evaluates (SPF is the other).
        Gmail and Yahoo's sender requirements make both effectively mandatory for bulk mail. Check
        your whole stack at once, SPF, DKIM, DMARC, DNSSEC, in the
        <a href="/">homepage security scanner</a>.
      </p>

      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/how-to-add-dkim-record/">How to add a DKIM record</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dkim-selector/">What is a DKIM selector?</a></li>
        <li><a href="/spf-record/">SPF explained</a> · <a href="/dmarc/">DMARC explained</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does DKIM encrypt email?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. DKIM signs the message so receivers can verify it was not altered. Encryption of the transport is a separate TLS mechanism.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if my DKIM key is revoked?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Mail signed with that selector fails verification. That is why rotation publishes the new selector first and retires the old one only after signing moves.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I have multiple DKIM records?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, under different selectors. Each selector is its own DNS name, and mail carries the selector it was signed with.</p></details></div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
